// ruya-devices (a user unit of ruya-shell, ADR-010) sets the console's time zone through
// systemd-timedated (org.ruyaos.Devices1.Time, SetTimezone). timedated asks polkit for it, and
// nobody is there to type a password on a TV, so exactly this action is allowed for the user
// ruya-shell:
//
//   org.freedesktop.timedate1.set-timezone
//
// Every other timedate1 action is refused outright for this user (NO, not "ask for a password":
// nobody could answer): the clock itself (set-time), the RTC mode (set-local-rtc) and NTP on or
// off (set-ntp). Other users (profile users, Flatpak apps) keep the default policy.
//
// Trust boundary, honestly: polkit sees the caller's user, not the profile. Any process running as
// ruya-shell can call timedate1.SetTimezone directly; the admin-profile rule in ruya-devices
// (time.rs, caller_may_set) decides what the shell's own UI offers and what the Devices1.Time door
// accepts, it is not a barrier against code that already runs as ruya-shell. That is the same
// boundary as the power, network and storage rules next to this one. Third-party apps never run as
// ruya-shell (Flatpak sandboxes, other users).
// scripts/vm-devices-check.sh asks pkcheck for the verdicts as the service's own process.
polkit.addRule(function(action, subject) {
    if (subject.user !== "ruya-shell")
        return polkit.Result.NOT_HANDLED;

    if (action.id === "org.freedesktop.timedate1.set-timezone")
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    if (action.id.indexOf("org.freedesktop.timedate1.") === 0)
        return polkit.Result.NO;
    return polkit.Result.NOT_HANDLED;
});
